{"name":"Aircraft Blue","hex":"#354f58","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aircraft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vehicle capable of atmospheric flight due to interaction with the air, such as buoyancy or lift.","One that can carry a person or persons."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aircraft"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aircraft","blocks":[{"text":"An aircraft or aerial vehicle is a vehicle that can fly by gaining support from the air. It counters the force of gravity by using either static lift or the dynamic lift of an airfoil, or, in a few cases, direct downward thrust from its engines. Common examples of aircraft include airplanes, drones, rotorcraft, airships, gliders, paramotors, and hot air balloons. Part 1 of Subchapter A of Chapter I of Title 14 of the U. S. Code of Federal Regulations states that aircraft \"means a device that is used or intended to be used for flight in the air\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Cessna_172S_Skyhawk_%E2%80%98G-JMKE%E2%80%99_%2845077563364%29.jpg/330px-Cessna_172S_Skyhawk_%E2%80%98G-JMKE%E2%80%99_%2845077563364%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aircraft","wikidata":"Q11436"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/RAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg/500px-RAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/RAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg/1280px-RAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/RAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg/500px-RAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Eurocopter AS350 Écureuil helicopter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARAN_squirrel_helicopter_at_melb_GP_08.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Aviation_%28Nouveaau_Larousse%2Cc._1900%29_DSCN2832.jpg/500px-Aviation_%28Nouveaau_Larousse%2Cc._1900%29_DSCN2832.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Aviation_%28Nouveaau_Larousse%2Cc._1900%29_DSCN2832.jpg/1280px-Aviation_%28Nouveaau_Larousse%2Cc._1900%29_DSCN2832.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=